.135 mol/m²·s·atm, J. 0.092 mol/m²·s·atm", "answer_ms": 3865}
{"id": "q0166", "pool": "hard-knowledge", "tier": "never", "audio_s": 24.26, "n_chunks": 25, "n_concurrent": 25, "gen_active_at_eot": true, "eot_score": 0.4314, "eot_read_ms": 21, "reference_answer": "C. the same", "query": "Consider two mercury barometers, one having a cross-sectional area of 1 cm2 and the other 2 cm2. The height of mercury in the narrower tube is\n\nA. two-thirds\nB. twice\nC. the same\nD. half\nE. four times higher\nF. depends on the atmospheric pressure\nG. three times higher\nH. one-third\nI. depends on the temperature\nJ. None of these\n\nAnswer with the correct option letter and its content.", "transcript": "Consider two mercury barometers, one having a cross-sectional area of one centimeters and the other two centimeters. The height of mercury in the narrower tube is (A) two-thirds, (B) twice, (C) the same, (D) half, (E) four times higher, (F) depends on the atmospheric pressure, (G) three times higher, (H) one-third, (I) depends on the temperature, (J) none of these. Answer with the correct option letter and its content.", "mode": "local", "answer": "C", "answer_ms": 43}
{"id": "q0175", "pool": "hard-knowledge", "tier": "never", "audio_s": 33.86, "n_chunks": 34, "n_concurrent": 34, "gen_active_at_eot": true, "eot_score": 0.3701, "eot_read_ms": 22, "reference_answer": "A. on the “honor system” only", "query": "At the outset of group counseling, the therapist should explain char the obligation of the group member to respect confidentiality is\n\nA. on the “honor system” only\nB. protected by state/provincial laws\nC. less of an issue than it is in individual therapy\nD. mandated by federal law\nE. the legal responsibility of the therapist\nF. only applicable to the therapist\nG. only necessary if sensitive information is shared\nH. dependent on the group's consensus\nI. not applicable in group settings\n\nAnswer with the correct option letter and its content.", "transcript": "At the outset of group counseling, the therapist should explain char the obligation of the group member to respect confidentiality is a on the honor system only b protected by state provincial laws c less of an issue than it is in individual therapy d mandated by federal law e the legal responsibility of the therapist f only applicable to the therapist g only necessary if sensitive information is shared h dependent on the group's consensus i not applicable in group settings Answer with the correct option letter and its content.", "mode": "local", "answer": "Answer with the correct option letter and its content.", "answer_ms": 247}
{"id": "q0191", "pool": "hard-knowledge", "tier": "never", "audio_s": 98.42, "n_chunks": 99, "n_concurrent": 99, "gen_active_at_eot": true, "eot_score": 0.3578, "eot_read_ms": 22, "reference_answer": "H. The nephew has a life estate, the niece has a life estate pur autre vie, and the gardener has a vested remainder.", "query": "An uncle owns his property in fee simple. The uncle executes and delivers a deed to the property with the following granting clause:\"To my nephew and my niece for the life of my nephew, then to my gardener. \"As a result of the grant, which of the following is the most accurate statement regarding the ownership rights of the property?\n\nA. The nephew has a life estate pur autre vie, the niece has a vested remainder, and the gardener has a life estate.\nB. The nephew and niece are tenants in common for the life of the first of them to die, the nephew and niece have contingent remainders in fee simple defeasible, and the gardener has an executory interest.\nC. The nephew and niece are joint tenants with rights of survivorship, and the gardener has a contingent remainder.\nD. The nephew and niece are tenants in common, and the gardener has a vested remainder in fee simple.\nE. The nephew and niece are joint tenants in fee simple defeasible, and the gardener has an executory interest.\nF. The nephew has a vested remainder, the niece has a life estate, and the gardener has a life estate pur autre vie.\nG. The nephew and niece both have life estates pur autre vie, and the gardener has a contingent remainder.\nH. The nephew has a life estate, the niece has a life estate pur autre vie, and the gardener has a vested remainder.\nI. The nephew has a life estate, the niece has a vested remainder, and the gardener has a life estate pur autre vie.\nJ. The nephew has a life estate, the niece also has a life estate, and the gardener has a vested remainder.\n\nAnswer with the correct option letter and its content.", "transcript": "An uncle owns his property in fee simple. The uncle executes and delivers a deed to the property with the following granting clause: \"To my nephew and my niece for the life of my nephew, then to my gardener.\" As a result of the grant, which of the following is the most accurate statement regarding the ownership rights of the property? (A) The nephew has a life estate pur autre vie, the niece has a vested remainder, and the gardener has a life estate. (B) The nephew and niece are tenants in common for the life of the first of them to die. The nephew and niece have contingent remainders in fee simple defeasible, and the gardener has an executory interest. (C) The nephew and niece are joint tenants with rights of survivorship, and the gardener has a contingent remainder. (D) The nephew and niece are tenants in common, and the gardener has a vested remainder in fee simple. (E) The nephew and niece are joint tenants in fee simple defeasible, and the gardener has an executory interest. (F) The nephew has a vested remainder, the niece has a life estate, and the gardener has a life estate pur autre vie. (G) The nephew and niece both have life estates pur autre vie, and the gardener has a contingent remainder. (H) The nephew has a life estate, the niece has a life estate pur autre vie, and the gardener has a vested remainder. (I) The nephew has a life estate, the niece has a vested remainder, and the gardener has a life estate pur autre vie. (J) The nephew has a life estate, the niece also has a life estate, and the gardener has a vested remainder. Answer with the correct option letter and its content.", "mode": "local", "answer": "Answer with the correct option letter and its content.", "answer_ms": 246}
{"id": "q0201", "pool": "hard-knowledge", "tier": "never", "audio_s": 39.56, "n_chunks": 40, "n_concurrent": 40, "gen_active_at_eot": true, "eot_score": 0.4749, "eot_read_ms": 23, "reference_answer": "J. False, False", "query": "Statement 1| Layer Normalization is used in the original ResNet paper, not Batch Normalization. Statement 2| DCGANs use self-attention to stabilize training.\n\nA. Statement 2 is True, but it's not clear about Statement 1\nB. Statement 1 and 2 are both partially true and partially false\nC. Statement 1 is False, but it's not clear about Statement 2\nD. False, True\nE. Statement 1 is True, but it's not clear about Statement 2\nF. True, True\nG. Statement 2 is False, but it's not clear about Statement 1\nH. True, False\nI. Both Statements are unclear\nJ. False, False\n\nAnswer with the correct option letter and its content.", "transcript": "Statement1: Layer normalization is used in the original ResNet paper, not batch normalization. Statement2: DCGANs use self-attention to stabilize training. A: Statement2 is true, but it's not clear about statement1. B: Statement1 and 2 are both partially true and partially false. C: Statement1 is false, but it's not clear about statement2. D: false true E: Statement1 is true, but it's not clear about statement2. F: true true G: Statement2 is false, but it's not clear about statement1 H: true false I: Both statements are unclear J: false false", "mode": "local", "answer": "E. Statement 1 is true, but it's not clear about statement 2.", "answer_ms": 448}
{"id": "q0212", "pool": "hard-knowledge", "tier": "never", "audio_s": 23.98, "n_chunks": 24, "n_concurrent": 24, "gen_active_at_eot": true, "eot_score": 0.5243, "eot_read_ms": 23, "reference_answer": "I. [z / (z - 1)]", "query": "Using the definition of the unilateral Z transform, F (z) = ^\\infty\\sum_n=0 f[n] z^-n. find the z - transform of the step-sequence f[n] = u[n].\n\nA. [1 / (z - 1)]\nB. [z / (1 + z)]\nC. [z^2 / (z - 1)]\nD. [1 / (2 - z)]\nE. [(z - 1) / z]\nF. [1 / (z^2 - 1)]\nG. [1 / (1 - z)]\nH. [z / (1 - z)]\nI. [z / (z - 1)]\nJ. [z / (z^2 - 1)]\n\nAnswer with the correct option letter and its content.", "transcript": "Using the definition of the unilateral z-transform, F(z)=sum_(n=0)^oo f(n)z^(-n), find the z-transform of the step sequence f(n)=u(n). a) (1-z^(-1))/(1+z^(-1)), b) (z-z^(-1))/(z-2), c) (z-z^(-1))/(1-z^(-1)), d) (1-z^(-1))/(1-z^(-1)), e) z/(1-z^(-1)), f) (1-z^(-1))/(1+z^(-1)), g) z/(1+z^(-1)), h) z/(z-1), i) z/(1-z^(-1)), j) z/(1+z^(-1)). Answer with the correct option letter and its content.", "mode": "local", "answer": "The Z-transform of the unit step sequence $ f[n]=u[n]\\ $ is given by option E, $ F[z]=\\frac{z}{z-1} $.", "answer_ms": 874}
{"id": "q0218", "pool": "hard-knowledge", "tier": "never", "audio_s": 16.71, "n_chunks": 17, "n_concurrent": 17, "gen_active_at_eot": true, "eot_score": 0.4217, "eot_read_ms": 24, "reference_answer": "C. price = minimum average variable cost.", "query": "Which of the following is not correct for the perfectly competitive firm, in the long run?\n\nA. price = marginal revenue.\nB. price = minimum average cost.\nC. price = minimum average variable cost.\nD. price = marginal cost.\n\nAnswer with the correct option letter and its content.", "transcript": "Which of the following is not correct for the perfectly competitive firm in the long run? a) Price equals marginal revenue. b) Price equals minimum average cost. c) Price minimum average variable cost. d) Price equals marginal cost. Answer with the correct option letter and its content:", "mode": "local", "answer": "C. Price = minimum average variable cost", "answer_ms": 221}
{"id": "q0229", "pool": "hard-knowledge", "tier": "never", "audio_s": 22.06, "n_chunks": 23, "n_concurrent": 23, "gen_active_at_eot": true, "eot_score": 0.4651, "eot_read_ms": 24, "reference_answer": "I. differential treatment.", "query": " According to Singer, the principle of equality requires\n\nA. differential indifference.\nB. unequal consideration.\nC. identical consideration.\nD. identical indifference.\nE. unequal treatment.\nF. equal consideration.\nG. equal treatment.\nH. identical treatment.\nI. differential treatment.\nJ. differential consideration.\n\nAnswer with the correct option letter and its content.", "transcript": "According to Singer, the principle of equality requires a differential indifference, b unequal consideration, c identical consideration, d identical indifference, e unequal treatment, f equal consideration, g equal treatment, h identical treatment, i differential treatment, j differential consideration.", "mode": "local", "answer": "Answer with the correct option letter and its content.", "answer_ms": 272}
{"id": "q0237", "pool": "hard-knowledge", "tier": "never", "audio_s": 95.91, "n_chunks": 96, "n_concurrent": 96, "gen_active_at_eot": true, "eot_score": 0.2821, "eot_read_ms": 24, "reference_answer": "J. Back pitch for lap winding is 9, front pitch is 8. Back pitch for wave winding is 9, front pitch is 9 or 10.", "query": "(a) A 4-pole machine has lap winding with 36 winding elements. What are the back and front pitches of this winding, expressed in commutator bars? (b) A 4-pole machine has wave winding with 37 winding elements. What are the three pitches of this winding expressed in commutator bars?\n\nA. Back pitch for lap winding is 12, front pitch is 13. Back pitch for wave winding is 7, front pitch is 6 or 7.\nB. Back pitch for lap winding is 9, front pitch is 10. Back pitch for wave winding is 9, front pitch is 8 or 9.\nC. Back pitch for lap winding is 8, front pitch is 9. Back pitch for wave winding is 10, front pitch is 11 or 12.\nD. Back pitch for both windings is 8, front pitch is 9 or 10.\nE. Back pitch for both windings is 9, front pitch is 11 or 12.\nF. Back pitch for lap winding is 11, front pitch is 9. Back pitch for wave winding is 10, front pitch is 8 or 9.\nG. Back pitch for lap winding is 7, front pitch is 6. Back pitch for wave winding is 11, front pitch is 12 or 13.\nH. Back pitch for lap winding is 10, front pitch is 8. Back pitch for wave winding is 9, front pitch is 9 or 10.\nI. Back pitch for lap winding is 10, front pitch is 11. Back pitch for wave winding is 8, front pitch is 7 or 8.\nJ. Back pitch for lap winding is 9, front pitch is 8. Back pitch for wave winding is 9, front pitch is 9 or 10.\n\nAnswer with the correct option letter and its content.", "transcript": "(a) Back pitch for lap winding is twelve, front pitch is thirteen. Back pitch for wave winding is seven, front pitch is six or seven. (b) Back pitch for lap winding is nine, front pitch is ten. Back pitch for wave winding is nine, front pitch is eight or nine. (c) Back pitch for lap winding is eight, front pitch is nine. Back pitch for wave winding is ten, front pitch is eleven or twelve. (d) Back pitch for both windings is eight, front pitch is nine or ten. (e) Back pitch for both windings is nine, front pitch is eleven or twelve. (f) Back pitch for lap winding is eleven, front pitch is nine. Back pitch for wave winding is ten, front pitch is eight or nine. (g) Back pitch for lap winding is seven, front pitch is six. Back pitch for wave winding is eleven, front pitch is twelve or thirteen. (h) Back pitch for lap winding is ten, front pitch is eight. Back pitch for wave winding is nine, front pitch is nine or ten. (i) Back pitch for lap winding is ten, front pitch is eleven. Back pitch for wave winding is eight, front pitch is seven or eight. (j) Back pitch for lap winding is nine, front pitch is eight. Back pitch for wave winding is nine, front pitch is nine or ten.", "mode": "local", "answer": "Answer with the correct option letter and its content.", "answer_ms": 273}
{"id": "q0242", "pool": "hard-knowledge", "tier": "never", "audio_s": 42.36, "n_chunks": 43, "n_concurrent": 43, "gen_active_at_eot": true, "eot_score": 0.2101, "eot_read_ms": 24, "reference_answer": "B. 65,000 feet", "query": "The lowest point on Earth is the bottom of the Mariana Trench at a depth of 35,840 feet below sea level. The highest point on Earth is the summit of Mt. Everest at a height of 29,028 feet above sea level. Which of the following is the best estimate of the distance between the lowest and highest points on Earth?\n\nA. 75,000 feet\nB. 65,000 feet\nC. 55,000 feet\nD. 60,000 feet\nE. 7,000 feet\nF. 64,000 feet\nG. 70,000 feet\nH. 50,000 feet\nI. 6,000 feet\nJ. 80,000 feet\n\nAnswer with the correct option letter and its content.", "transcript": "The lowest point on Earth is the bottom of the Mariana Trench at a depth of thirty five thousand eight hundred and forty feet below sea level. The highest point on Earth is the summit of Mount Everest at a height of twenty nine thousand twenty eight feet above sea level. Which of the following is the best estimate of the distance between the lowest and highest points on Earth? (A) seventy five thousand feet, (B) sixty five thousand feet, (C) fifty five thousand feet, (D) sixty thousand feet, (E) seven thousand feet, (F) sixty four thousand feet, (G) seventy thousand feet, (H) fifty thousand feet, (I) six thousand feet, (J) eighty thousand feet. Answer with the correct option letter and its content.", "mode": "local", "answer": "B. 65,000 feet", "answer_ms": 270}
{"id": "q0248", "pool": "hard-knowledge", "tier": "never", "audio_s": 13.84, "n_chunks": 14, "n_concurrent": 14, "gen_active_at_eot": true, "eot_score": 0.3912, "eot_read_ms": 24, "reference_answer": "A. Lhf", "query": "Select the best translation into predicate logic. Holly loves Frances. (f: Frances; h: Holly; Lxy: x loves y)\n\nA. Lhf\nB. hLf\nC. hLh\nD. Lfh\nE. LfLh\nF. fLhL\nG. fLf\nH. hLfL\nI. fLh\nJ. LfhL\n\nAnswer with the correct option letter and its content.", "transcript": "Holly loves Frances. Frances H. Holly. ∀x∀y (L(x,y)). A) ∀x∀y (L(x,y)). B) ∃x∃y (L(x,y)). C) ∃x∀y (L(x,y)). D) ∀x∃y (L(x,y)).", "mode": "local", "answer": "A. ∀x LgHx → Fx", "answer_ms": 297}
{"id": "q0256", "pool": "hard-knowledge", "tier": "never", "audio_s": 58.91, "n_chunks": 59, "n_concurrent": 59, "gen_active_at_eot": true, "eot_score": 0.6245, "eot_read_ms": 27, "reference_answer": "B. i_forced= 24cos1000t - 12 sin 1000tmA", "query": "A voltage source 60cos1000t V is in series with a 2-k\\Omega resistor and a 1 -\\muFcapacitor. Findi_forced.\n\nA. i_forced= 28cos1000t - 14 sin 1000tmA\nB. i_forced= 24cos1000t - 12 sin 1000tmA\nC. i_forced= 20cos1000t + 10 sin 1000tmA\nD. i_forced= 30cos1000t - 15 sin 1000tmA\nE. i_forced= 15cos1000t - 30 sin 1000tmA\nF. i_forced= 30cos1000t + 15 sin 1000tmA\nG. i_forced= 24cos1000t + 12 sin 1000tmA\nH. i_forced= 20cos1000t - 10 sin 1000tmA\nI. i_forced= 26cos1000t - 13 sin 1000tmA\nJ. i_forced= 28cos1000t + 14 sin 1000tmA\n\nAnswer with the correct option letter and its content.", "transcript": "A voltage source 60 cos(1000t) V is in series with a 2 kΩ resistor and a 1-μF capacitor. Find iforce. (a) iforce = 28 cos(1000t) - 14 sin(1000t) mA. (b) iforce = 24 cos(1000t) + 12 sin(1000t) mA. (c) iforce = 20 cos(1000t) + 10 sin(1000t) mA. (d) iforce = 30 cos(1000t) - 15 sin(1000t) mA. (e) iforce = 15 cos(1000t) - 30 sin(1000t) mA. (f) iforce = 30 cos(1000t) + 15 sin(1000t) mA. (g) iforce = 24 cos(1000t) + 12 sin(1000t) mA. (h) iforce = 20 cos(1000t) - 10 sin(1000t) mA. (i) iforce = 26 cos(1000t) - 13 sin(1000t) mA. (j) iforce = 28 cos(1000t) + 14 sin(1000t) mA. Answer with the correct option letter and its content.", "mode": "local", "answer": "The correct answer is B. i_f = 24cos1000t + 12sin1000t mA.", "answer_ms": 863}
{"id": "q0268", "pool": "hard-knowledge", "tier": "never", "audio_s": 20.16, "n_chunks": 21, "n_concurrent": 21, "gen_active_at_eot": true, "eot_score": 0.4863, "eot_read_ms": 25, "reference_answer": "F. 20%", "query": "The percentage of children in Vietnam (age 8) who reported physical punishment by teachers in the past week in 2009 was about what?\n\nA. 60%\nB. 30%\nC. 10%\nD. 40%\nE. 70%\nF. 20%\nG. 90%\nH. 50%\nI. 80%\nJ. 100%\n\nAnswer with the correct option letter and its content.", "transcript": "The percentage of children in Vietnam aged eight who reported physical punishment by teachers in the past week in two thousand nine was about what? (A) sixty percent, (B) thirty percent, (C) ten percent, (D) forty percent, (E) seventy percent, (F) twenty percent, (G) ninety percent, (H) fifty percent, (I) eighty percent, (J) one hundred percent.", "mode": "local", "answer": "Answer is A, 60%.", "answer_ms": 230}
{"id": "q0273", "pool": "hard-knowledge", "tier": "never", "audio_s": 23.07, "n_chunks": 24, "n_concurrent": 24, "gen_active_at_eot": true, "eot_score": 0.2829, "eot_read_ms": 24, "reference_answer": "D. x = 3 and x = 2", "query": "Use the quadratic formula to solve for x in the equation x^2 - 5x + 6 = 0.\n\nA. x = -1 and x = 6\nB. x = 0 and x = 6\nC. x = 1 and x = 2\nD. x = 3 and x = 2\nE. x = 1 and x = 6\nF. x = 3 and x = 4\nG. x = 2.5 and x = -2.4\nH. x = -2 and x = -3\nI. x = 4 and x = 5\n\nAnswer with the correct option letter and its content.", "transcript": "Use the quadratic formula to solve for x in the equation x squared minus five x plus six equals zero. a) x equals minus one and x equals six, b) x equals zero and x equals six, c) x equals one and x equals three and x equals two, d) x equals one and x equals six, f) x equals three and x equals four, g) x equals two pi and x equals minus two pi, h) x equals minus two and x equals three, i) x equals four and x equals five. Answer with the correct option letter and its content.", "mode": "local", "answer": "C", "answer_ms": 51}
{"id": "q0288", "pool": "hard-knowledge", "tier": "never", "audio_s": 115.11, "n_chunks": 116, "n_concurrent": 116, "gen_active_at_eot": true, "eot_score": 0.4937, "eot_read_ms": 24, "reference_answer": "G. Yes, it must be suppressed because she was secured in the squad car and the police could not expect to find evidence relating to the current arrest in the car.", "query": "A highway patrol officer stopped a driver for speeding and found that the person had a suspended license. He allowed her husband to drive the car home but arrested her for driving under suspension. After securing her inside the squad car, he conducted a search of her vehicle. The officer found a bag of cocaine inside a zippered jacket on the back seat. He arrested her for possession and possession with intent to deliver a controlled substance. Prior to trial, the driver moved for suppression of the cocaine. Is it likely that the trial court will grant the suppression motion?\n\nA. Yes, the motion will be granted because a search of a vehicle requires a search warrant unless there are exceptional exigent circumstances.\nB. Yes, the motion will be granted because the officer did not have the driver's consent to search the car.\nC. No, the motion will be denied because the officer had the right to search the car for officer safety reasons.\nD. No, the motion will be denied because the officer had a reasonable suspicion to search the car due to the driver's suspended license.\nE. No, the motion will be denied because the officer had probable cause to search the car after finding out about the suspended license.\nF. No, the motion will be denied because this was a valid search incident to an arrest.\nG. Yes, it must be suppressed because she was secured in the squad car and the police could not expect to find evidence relating to the current arrest in the car.\nH. Yes, the motion will be granted because the cocaine was found in a personal item, not in plain sight.\nI. No, the motion will be denied because there is no expectation of privacy toward property inside one's car.\nJ. Yes, the motion will be granted because the officer did not have a warrant to search the jacket on the back seat.\n\nAnswer with the correct option letter and its content.", "transcript": "A highway patrol officer stopped a driver for speeding and found that the person had a suspended license. He allowed her husband to drive the car home but arrested her for driving under suspension. After securing her inside the squad car, he conducted a search of her vehicle. The officer found a bag of cocaine inside a zippered jacket on the back seat. He arrested her for possession and possession with intent to deliver a controlled substance. Prior to trial, the driver moved for suppression of the cocaine. Is it likely that the trial court will grant the suppression motion? A. Yes, the motion will be granted because a search of a vehicle requires a search warrant unless there are exceptional exigent circumstances. B. Yes, the motion will be granted because the officer did not have the driver's consent to search the car. C. No, the motion will be denied because the officer had the right to search the car for officer safety reasons. D. No, the motion will be denied because the officer had a reasonable suspicion to search the car due to the driver's suspended license. E. No, the motion will be denied because the officer had probable cause to search the car after finding out about the suspended license. F. No, the motion will be denied because this was a valid search incident to an arrest. G. Yes, it must be suppressed because she was secured in the squad car and the police could not expect to find evidence relating to the current arrest in the car. H. Yes, the motion will be granted because the cocaine was found in a personal item not in plain sight. I. No, the motion will be denied because there is no expectation of privacy toward property inside one's car. J. Yes, the motion will be granted because the officer did not have a warrant to search the jacket on the back seat.", "mode": "local", "answer": "Answer B", "answer_ms": 75}
